Your pipeline currently probably uses a hardcoded S3 URI for input. To make it dynamic, you need to add a pipeline parameter and use it in the processing step.

1.1 Add the Parameter Definition
In your pipeline definition script (e.g., the one you run to upsert the pipeline), add:


###########################################
python
from sagemaker.workflow.parameters import ParameterString

input_data_param = ParameterString(
    name="InputDataUri",                          # Name used in EventBridge
    default_value="s3://main-sagemaker-ml-healthcare/mlops/diabetes.csv"  # fallback
)


############################################################

1.2 Use the Parameter in the Processing Step
Locate your ProcessingStep (or wherever you read the input file). Replace the hardcoded source with input_data_param:

python
ProcessingInput(
    source=input_data_param,
    destination="/opt/ml/processing/input",
    ...
)

######################################################################################

1.3 Include the Parameter in the Pipeline Definition
When you instantiate the Pipeline, add the parameter to the parameters list:

python
pipeline = Pipeline(
    name="eventbridge-triggering",
    parameters=[input_data_param],
    steps=[...],
    sagemaker_session=pipeline_session
)

#########################################################################################


Step 2: Update the Pipeline in SageMaker
Run your pipeline script (the one with the modifications) once to upsert the new definition:

python
pipeline.upsert(role_arn=role)
This registers the parameter in SageMaker. Now your pipeline is ready to accept a dynamic InputDataUri value.

########################################################################################

3.5 Configure Input Transformation (Critical)
You must pass the S3 URI of the newly uploaded file to the pipeline parameter. Enable Input transformation and fill:

Input Path
Extract bucket name and object key:

json
{
  "bucket": "$.detail.bucket.name",
  "key": "$.detail.object.key"
}
#########################################################################################

Input Template
Build the S3 URI and map it to the pipeline parameter InputDataUri:

json
{
  "InputDataUri": "s3://<bucket>/<key>"
}
(Some versions of the console require "s3://$.bucket/$.key". Try the first; if it fails, use the second.)

Important: The parameter name in the template must exactly match the name you used in your pipeline code (InputDataUri).

############################################################################################

3.6 Set Up IAM Role
EventBridge needs permission to start your pipeline.

Under Role, choose Create a new role for this specific rule (recommended). The console will automatically create a role with the necessary trust policy and permission sagemaker:StartPipelineExecution on your pipeline ARN.

If you prefer an existing role, ensure it has a policy like:

json
{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": "sagemaker:StartPipelineExecution",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:sagemaker:ap-south-1:123456789012:pipeline/eventbridge-triggering"
}
and a trust policy for events.amazonaws.com.


##################################################################################################

3.7 Create the Rule
Click Create. The rule is now active and will trigger on every matching S3 upload.

#################################################################################################
Step 4: Test the Automation
Upload a test CSV file to your bucket (e.g., s3://main-sagemaker-ml-healthcare/mlops/test-data.csv).

Go to SageMaker Studio → Pipelines → select your pipeline.

Look for a new execution. It may take a minute to appear.

Click the execution and go to the Parameters tab. You should see InputDataUri with the value s3://main-sagemaker-ml-healthcare/mlops/test-data.csv.

Verify the pipeline steps run successfully and process that file.
